import{_ as e,o,c as d,ao as a}from"./chunks/framework.C_54s4mn.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"GPU compatibility","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"compatibility/gpu.md","filePath":"compatibility/gpu.md","lastUpdated":null}'),l={name:"compatibility/gpu.md"};function n(i,t,r,c,s,h){return o(),d("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[a('<h1 id="GPU-compatibility" tabindex="-1">GPU compatibility <a class="header-anchor" href="#GPU-compatibility" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;GPU compatibility {#GPU-compatibility}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Which <code>Flow</code> constructors and AD backends work with a device state (<code>CuArray</code>), on NVIDIA/CUDA through CUDA.jl. AMDGPU and Metal are untested. The <a href="./../flows/gpu">GPU flows</a> guide explains the contract behind these results: no scalar indexing, <code>method=:gpu</code>, the choice of AD backend.</p><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Measured on a device, not executed by the documentation build</p><p>The documentation build has no GPU, so the code blocks of this page are plain <code>julia</code> blocks. The results come from a run of <a href="https://github.com/control-toolbox/CTFlows.jl/blob/main/probe/gpu/probe_gpu.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>probe/gpu/probe_gpu.jl</code></a> on the self-hosted NVIDIA runner (<a href="https://github.com/control-toolbox/CTFlows.jl/actions/runs/30037105763/job/89317162708" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">run 30037105763</a>, 2026-07-23). To check them against the current code, add the <code>run probe</code> label to a pull request.</p></div><hr><h2 id="Per-constructor-table" tabindex="-1">Per-constructor table <a class="header-anchor" href="#Per-constructor-table" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Per-constructor table {#Per-constructor-table}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>&quot;Probe&quot; says whether <code>probe/gpu</code> measures the row; the other rows rest on the hardware tests of the test suite (see <a href="/CTFlows.jl/dev/flows/gpu#What-runs-on-device">GPU flows</a>).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Flow source</th><th style="text-align:right;">AD needed</th><th style="text-align:right;">Device call</th><th style="text-align:right;">Probe</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>Flow(::VectorField)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">no</td><td style="text-align:right;">pass <code>CuArray</code>s</td><td style="text-align:right;">✓ point and trajectory</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>Flow(::HamiltonianVectorField)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">no</td><td style="text-align:right;">pass <code>CuArray</code>s</td><td style="text-align:right;">✓ point</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>Flow(::ODEFunction)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">no</td><td style="text-align:right;">pass <code>CuArray</code>s</td><td style="text-align:right;">not measured; same mechanism as <code>Flow(VectorField)</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>Flow(::ODEProblem)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">no</td><td style="text-align:right;">pass <code>CuArray</code>s</td><td style="text-align:right;">✓ point (<code>remake</code>)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>Flow(ocp)</code>, state call (no costate)</td><td style="text-align:right;">no</td><td style="text-align:right;">pass <code>CuArray</code>s</td><td style="text-align:right;">not measured</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>Flow(ocp, OpenLoop/ClosedLoop)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">no</td><td style="text-align:right;">pass <code>CuArray</code>s</td><td style="text-align:right;">not measured</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>Flow(::Hamiltonian)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">yes</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>method=:gpu</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">✓ <code>AutoZygote</code> and <code>AutoMooncake</code>; the CPU default fails (scalar indexing)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>Flow(ocp)</code>, Hamiltonian call / <code>Flow(ocp, DynClosedLoop)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">yes</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>method=:gpu</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">✓ <code>AutoMooncake</code> only (in-place OCP dynamics)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Multi-phase concatenation (<code>*</code>), with jumps</td><td style="text-align:right;">inherits</td><td style="text-align:right;">works on device</td><td style="text-align:right;">not measured</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>variable_costate=true</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">yes</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>ad_backend=AutoZygote()</code>, host or device <code>variable</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">✓ host variable, device variable of length 1 and 2</td></tr></tbody></table><hr><h2 id="AD-backends-on-device" tabindex="-1">AD backends on device <a class="header-anchor" href="#AD-backends-on-device" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;AD backends on device {#AD-backends-on-device}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Four AD backends, on four call shapes: a plain gradient, the gradient of a <code>Hamiltonian</code>, a <strong>mutating</strong> in-place right-hand side, and the Hamiltonian of a <code>Flow(ocp)</code> (in-place under the hood, through the <code>dynamics!</code> of CTModels):</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Backend</th><th style="text-align:center;">Plain gradient</th><th style="text-align:center;"><code>hamiltonian_gradient</code></th><th style="text-align:center;">Mutating in-place RHS</th><th style="text-align:center;"><code>Flow(ocp)</code> Hamiltonian</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>AutoForwardDiff</code> (CPU default)</td><td style="text-align:center;">✗ scalar indexing</td><td style="text-align:center;">✗ scalar indexing</td><td style="text-align:center;">—</td><td style="text-align:center;">—</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>AutoZygote</code></td><td style="text-align:center;">✓</td><td style="text-align:center;">✗ <code>KernelException</code></td><td style="text-align:center;">✗ can&#39;t differentiate <code>gc_preserve_end</code></td><td style="text-align:center;">✗ can&#39;t differentiate <code>gc_preserve_end</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>AutoMooncake</code> (GPU default)</td><td style="text-align:center;">✓</td><td style="text-align:center;">✓</td><td style="text-align:center;">✓</td><td style="text-align:center;">✓</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>AutoEnzyme</code></td><td style="text-align:center;">✗ <code>EnzymeRuntimeActivityError</code></td><td style="text-align:center;">✗ <code>EnzymeMutabilityException</code></td><td style="text-align:center;">✓</td><td style="text-align:center;">✗ <code>EnzymeMutabilityException</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>AutoMooncake</code> handles every call shape, which is why <code>method=:gpu</code> selects it.</p><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title"><code>AutoZygote</code> fails once the call reduces over a device array</p><p>The plain gradient works. Through <code>hamiltonian_gradient</code> (one more <code>sum(abs2, …)</code> layer) it raises a <code>KernelException</code>, and with mutation it cannot differentiate <code>gc_preserve_end</code> (a mutation-tracking marker).</p></div><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title"><code>AutoEnzyme</code> fails on GPU array reductions, not on mutation</p><p>A mutating right-hand side with no reduction (<code>du .= 2 .* u</code>) differentiates, on host and on device. Every failure in the table comes from a <code>sum</code>/<code>mapreduce</code> over a <code>CuArray</code> inside the traced function. Real Hamiltonians contain such reductions (<code>sum(abs2, x)</code>, <code>dot(p, x)</code>), so <code>AutoEnzyme</code> is not usable for <code>Flow(::Hamiltonian)</code> or <code>Flow(ocp, …)</code> on device.</p></div><hr><h2 id="Ensembles:-a-coupled-system-needs-the-kernel-path" tabindex="-1">Ensembles: a coupled system needs the kernel path <a class="header-anchor" href="#Ensembles:-a-coupled-system-needs-the-kernel-path" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Ensembles: a coupled system needs the kernel path {#Ensembles:-a-coupled-system-needs-the-kernel-path}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><a href="/CTFlows.jl/dev/flows/gpu#GPU-ensembles">GPU ensembles</a> uses both DiffEqGPU.jl algorithms on a decoupled right-hand side. With a coupled one (<code>du .= A * u</code>, a matrix–vector product):</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Algorithm</th><th style="text-align:center;">Decoupled (<code>du .= .-u</code>)</th><th style="text-align:center;">Coupled (<code>du .= A * u</code>)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>EnsembleGPUArray</code> (in-place, host closures)</td><td style="text-align:center;">✓</td><td style="text-align:center;">✗ <code>InvalidIRError</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>EnsembleGPUKernel</code> (out-of-place <code>SVector</code>)</td><td style="text-align:center;">✓</td><td style="text-align:center;">✓</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The in-place kernel of <code>EnsembleGPUArray</code> cannot compile a BLAS matrix–vector product (kernels can neither allocate nor call BLAS). The out-of-place <code>SVector</code> right-hand side of <code>EnsembleGPUKernel</code> lowers a small static product to plain kernel code.</p><hr><h2 id="See-also" tabindex="-1">See also <a class="header-anchor" href="#See-also" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;See also {#See-also}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p><a href="./../flows/gpu">GPU flows</a>: the GPU contract for your own code, <code>method=:gpu</code>, AD backends, ensembles, testing on a device.</p></li><li><p><a href="./../dev/gpu-internals">GPU internals</a>: design decisions, the routing path, the coercion helpers.</p></li><li><p><a href="https://github.com/control-toolbox/CTFlows.jl/tree/main/probe/gpu" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>probe/gpu</code></a>: the probe behind this page.</p></li><li><p><a href="./overview">Compatibility</a>: the CPU pages.</p></li></ul>',22)])])}const p=e(l,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,p as default};
